{:nav-title "Predicates"
 :content
 [:div
  [:h1 "Filtering with Predicates"]
  [:p "So far we matched patterns by specifying exact values like "
   [:code "\"Bulbasaur\""] ". Predicates let us filter rows using arbitrary truthy expressions."]
  [:p "A predicate clause is a function call wrapped in an extra pair of brackets:"]
  [:ui/code "[( > ?speed 100 )]"]
  [:p "Every variable used in the predicate must already be bound by an earlier pattern clause. "
   "Let's find all Pokemon with a speed stat above 100:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name ?speed
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [?e :stat/speed ?speed]
    [(> ?speed 100)]]]
  [:p "We can compare two bound variables against each other. "
   "Here are the Pokemon whose defense is strictly higher than their attack:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name ?attack ?defense
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [?e :stat/attack ?attack]
    [?e :stat/defense ?defense]
    [(> ?defense ?attack)]]]
  [:p [:code "not="] " is a predicate too: " [:code "[(not= ?a ?b)]"] " keeps a row only if the two values differ. "
   "The next chapter puts it to use."]
  [:p "Predicates work on strings too, this site allows "
   [:code "clojure.string/starts-with?"] " and " [:code "clojure.string/includes?"]
   "; in a real Datomic app, any Clojure function is available. "
   "Here we find Pokemon whose name starts with \"S\":"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [(clojure.string/starts-with? ?name "S")]]]
  [:p [:strong "Good to know: "] [:code "="] ", " [:code "!="] ", " [:code "<"] ", " [:code "<="] ", "
   [:code ">"] " and " [:code ">="] " are "
   [:a {:href "https://docs.datomic.com/query/query-data-reference.html#range-predicates"} "range predicates"]
   ". Datomic answers them with an index instead of checking every value, "
   "so they are much more efficient than the same test written with another function. "
   "Datomic's own "
   [:a {:href "https://docs.datomic.com/query/query-data-reference.html#built-in-predicates-and-functions"} "built-in functions"]
   " are also cheaper than your own, since they run inside the query engine. "
   "Our database is too small to notice, but this matters in a real one. "
   "Strings sort in dictionary order, so the names starting with \"S\" are also the names from \"S\" up to, "
   "but not including, \"T\". That is the same "
   [:ui/value "pokemon" [:find (count ?e) . :where [?e :pokemon/name ?name] [(clojure.string/starts-with? ?name "S")]]]
   " Pokemon as before:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [(>= ?name "S")]
    [(< ?name "T")]]]
  [:p "Or find Pokemon whose name contains \"saur\":"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [(clojure.string/includes? ?name "saur")]]]
  [:ui/try-tip
   [:p "Combine two predicates to find Pokemon whose name starts with \"S\" "
    [:em "and"] " whose speed is above 100. "
    "Or find Pokemon whose attack " [:em "and"] " defense are both above 80."]]]}
